'''white'''<sup>1</sup> (hwīt), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< ME. ''whit, whyt, qvit, hwit,'' < AS. ''hwīt'' = OS. ''hwīt'' = OFries. ''hwīt'' = D. ''wit'' = LG. ''wit'' = OHG. MHG. ''wīz'', G. ''weiss'' = Icel. ''hvītr'' = Sw. ''hvit'' = Dan. ''hvid'' = Goth. ''hweits'', white; akin to Skt. ''çveta'', white, < √ ''çvit'', be white, shine: cf. ''çvitra, çvitna'', white, OBulg. ''svietŭ'', light, ''svĭtieti'', shine, give light, Russ. ''svietŭ'', light, etc. Hence ult. ''wheat, whitster, whittle''<sup>1</sup>, ''whiting''<sup>1</sup>, etc.] '''I.''' ''a.'' '''1.''' Of the color of pure snow or any powder of material transmitting all visible rays without sensible absorption; transmitting and so reflecting to the eye all the rays of the spectrum combined in the same proportions as in the impinging light, and thus, as seen in sunlight, conveying the same

readings.c2s3:
impression to the eye as sunlight of moderate intensity; not tinged or tinted with any of the proper colors or their compounds; snowy: the opposite of ''black'' or ''dark''.
{{smaller block|<poem>Amidde a tree fordrye, as ''whyte'' as chalk, . . .
Ther sat a faucon over hir heed ful hye.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Squire’s Tale, l. 401.}}
Fresshe lampraye bake; open y<sup>e</sup> pasty, than take ''whyte'' brede, and cut it thynne, & lay it in a dysshe.
{{right|''Babees Book'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 281.}}
<poem>A head
So old and ''white'' as this. ''Shak.'', Lear, iii. 2. 24.</poem>
<poem>Nor ever falls the least ''white'' star of snow.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Lucretius.}}}}
'''2.''' Pale; pallid; bloodless, as from fear or cowardice.
{{smaller block|<poem>To turn ''white'' and swoon at tragic shows.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Lover’s Complaint, l. 308.}}
<poem>Or whispering with ''white'' lips—“The foe! they come!”</poem>
{{right|''Byron'', Childe Harold, iii. 25.}}}}
'''3.''' Free from spot or guilt; pure; clean; stainless.
{{smaller block|<poem>Calumny
The ''whitest'' virtue strikes.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', M. for M., iii. 2. 198.}}
<poem>In the ''white'' way of virtue and true valour
You have been a pilgrim long.</poem>
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', Knight of Malta, ii. 5.}}}}
'''4†.''' Fair; beautiful.
{{smaller block|<poem>“Ye, ywis,” quod fresshe Antigone the ''white''.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Troilus, ii. 887.}}}}

